// Input which is not valid UTF-8. The byte 0xFF can occur in no UTF-8 encoding at all and 0x80 is a continuation byte
// with no leading byte in front of it, so no rule can ever match either of them and both are errors. A backend which
// decodes the input into its own string type first, rather than scanning the bytes, either replaces these bytes or
// refuses the input, and its offsets stop lining up with the ones here.
//
// The parser is not what this case is about, so the grammar is the single empty production the file format requires.

@scanner {
    LETTERS: /[a-z]+/;
}

@parser {
    tokens
        : @empty
        ;
}
